s supporting a slight nudge above the consensus figure.", "watch": "The margin could narrow if Marker's write-in campaign attracts unexpected funding or attention, but nothing in the current evidence suggests that is happening.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Incumbent Ben Ray Luján (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Senator Ben Ray Luján seeks a second term in a state that leans Democratic, facing Republican Larry Marker, who has run a minimal campaign. Every major rating, from Cook and Sabato to the Economist, places the contest in the Safe or Solid Democratic column, and the lone published poll, from Research & Polling in late August, showed Luján ahead 53 to 38 among likely voters. Betting markets put Democratic odds between 97 and 99 percent.\n\nThe financial picture is lopsided: Luján has raised $5.6 million and holds $4.4 million on hand, while Marker has raised about $34,000, with no outside spending against the incumbent reported. Headlines indicate Marker may be running as a write-in candidate, further limiting his visibility. With no competitive primary on the Democratic side and no recent negative developments for the incumbent, the race shows little evidence of vulnerability.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9923, "margin": 15.81}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9906, "margin": 15.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9896, "margin": 15.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9897, "margin": 15.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9862, "margin": 15.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.9853, "margin": 16.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.9894, "margin": 15.5}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.9908, "margin": 15.5}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.9915, "margin": 16.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.9926, "margin": 16.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.9922, "margin": 16.0}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.9884, "margin": 16.0}, {"date": "2026-10-04", "p_dem": 0.9935, "margin": 17.0}]}